# local-checks.conf — typed checks fleet-local-check runs on THIS node (things a remote
# health page can't reach). Copy to $HOME/.config/fleet-monitoring/local-checks.conf and edit.
#
#   type | name | arg …
#     service | <name> | <launchd-label | systemd-unit>     is that service running?
#     mount   | <name> | <path>                             is <path> a mount? (mount point + table)
#     http    | <name> | <url>                              200 == ok (e.g. cross-ping your Gatus)
#     command | <name> | <shell command>                    exit 0 = OK · exit 3 = UNKNOWN · else FAIL
#     hash    | <name> | <installed file> | <reference>     installed copy identical to its reference?
#     synclag | <name> | <git dir> | <max hours>             last successful fetch within <max hours>?
#
# Every check has THREE outcomes: OK, FAIL, or UNKNOWN (the probe could not answer — a timeout, an
# unreadable table, a command exiting 3). UNKNOWN keeps the previous state and sends no mail; four in
# a row become a FAIL that says "could not determine state" (guide § 17). A line that doesn't parse is
# reported as a failing check, never silently skipped.
#
# Replace these examples with your fleet's local-only concerns. Notes:
#  - `mount` never reads INSIDE the share — on macOS an NFS/SMB share is bound to the GUI login
#    session, so ls/stat inside it HANGS from a launchd/background run even when healthy. It checks
#    the mount point + table (retried; an unreadable table is UNKNOWN, not "unmounted"). It catches
#    "unmounted", not "hung". For hung-detection use a `command` via a login shell, e.g.:
#       command | media reachable | ssh localhost 'ls /path/to/mount >/dev/null'
#  - `hash` watches a file your config manager does NOT deploy — a script installed by hand on a node
#    it doesn't manage falls behind the repo silently otherwise. Point <reference> at the repo copy.
#  - `synclag` watches that pull-based sync is actually happening: a status command that never
#    fetches reports "clean" while days behind (guide 05b).
#  - `command` is the escape hatch: assert an app's workflows are active, a batch job exited 0,
#    a container is healthy — anything a shell one-liner can decide.
#  - a `service` with NO listening port (an outbound bridge/agent) is exactly what a remote
#    prober misses — check it here.

service | bridge daemon    | com.example.some-bridge
mount   | media share      | /path/to/your/mount
http    | gatus cross-ping | https://<gatus-host>/
# command | workflows active | test "$(sqlite3 ~/.app/db 'select count(*) from wf where active=1')" -ge 1
# command | batch last-run   | test "$(docker inspect -f '{{.State.ExitCode}}' <container>)" = 0
# hash    | gateway relay    | /usr/local/lib/<you>/relay | ~/.local/share/chezmoi/<path>/relay
# synclag | dotfile sync     | ~/.local/share/chezmoi | 3
